  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/c/ce/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_5.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "JJJ has sponsored his own TV program to discredit Spider-Man, which Peter Parker and various classmates watch from a bowling alley.&nbsp; But \"in another part of town,\" we see that Doctor Doom is also watching.&nbsp; As the Fantastic Four have always managed to frustrate his plans, perhaps if he allied himself with one such as Spider-Man...\n\nNow headquartered in a new lab, he uses a captured spider to relay a signal to Spider-Man, who he believes will pick up the message with his spider senses.&nbsp; It directs Spider-Man to him straight away, and Doom attempts to recruit him...unsuccessfully of course.&nbsp; After a very brief scuffle, Spider-Man escapes, and Doom prepares for Plan B.&nbsp; He blows up the \"deserted building\" he was hiding out in, making it look like Spider-Man is fleeing the scene.&nbsp; Of <i>course</i> he'll be blamed for the fire!&nbsp; But Doom is planning to find out who Spider-Man really is by devising a device that will react only to a spider's impulses and lead him directly to his prey.\n\nMeanwhile, the kids at Peter's school are laughing at Flash Thompson dressed in a Spider-Man costume to surprise Peter with.&nbsp; He doesn't know that at the same time, Dr. Doom has created a device to track Spider-Man's impulses, which he believes will lead him directly to the hero's true identity.\n\nAfter school, Flash (still in his homemade costume) prepares to jump out and scare \"puny Parker,\" but because Flash and Peter are so near each other, when Doom's device draws him to the area, Doom mistakes Flash for the real Spider-Man and captures him, hauling him off into his helicopter.\n\nWhen Peter finally reaches home, he finds Aunt May watching the Ed Sullivan show, but the broadcast is suddenly hijacked by Dr. Doom himself, who uses the television airwaves to issue a challenge aimed at the Fantastic Four.&nbsp; He indicates he'll kill Spider-Man unless the FF disband and surrender to him, giving them one hour to respond!&nbsp; Peter can't believe what he's seeing on the TV -- who <i>is</i> this fake Spider-Man?\n\nThe next moment, the Parker telephone rings, and Peter finds that it's Liz Allan, who, oddly enough, calls Peter to let him know that Flash was out dressed as Spider-Man and is now missing (<i>why would she do this?</i>).&nbsp; Peter puts two and two together and realizes that Doom has captured a fake Spider-Man, in reality Flash Thompson, whose life is now in mortal peril.\n\nKnowing he can't sit idly by and do nothing, Peter has no choice but to go out and hunt for Doom.&nbsp; He reasons that taking over the television airwaves probably required a lot of power, and so he heads to various areas of the city \"where some enormous dynamos may be located,\" hoping that once nearby, his spider senses will do the rest.\n\nFinally sensing \"hostile emanations\" coming from an abandoned factory (<i>Who abandons a factory in NYC</i>?), he swoops down and slithers through an air vent, hoping to avoid the traps he's <i>sure</i> Doom has laid in wait.&nbsp; And soon, Spider-Man engages Doom, who discovers he's kidnapped the wrong person!\n\nThe two battle for several pages, with Spider-Man constantly on the defense as Doom and his various devices gradually gain ground.&nbsp; Even Spider-Man can sense that he's barely hanging on and can't take much more, but the sudden arrival of the Fantastic Four causes Doom to realize he can't take on both Spider-Man and the FF at the same time.&nbsp; He flees the scene.&nbsp; Spider-Man also rushes away because he realizes he left Aunt May alone, leaving the arriving FF to rescue Flash Thompson.\n\nThe last panels show Peter with Aunt May, the next day at the offices of the <i>Bugle</i>, where JJJ berates him for not having any photos to sell, while Betty Brant tells him not to worry about it -- <i>she</i> thinks he's wonderful!&nbsp; And then finally at school where Flash Thompson is talking big about his \"escape\" from Doctor Doom.&nbsp; Peter's luck at school is true to form!",
  "grade_one_liner": "A mismatched Dr. Doom/Spider-Man showdown that is ambitious despite some of its odd choices and timing problems that keep tugging at your sleeve.",
  "subtitle": "When Doom Recruits (and Kidnaps) the Wrong Guy",
  "review_text": "A solid offering, pitting Spider-Man against Doctor Doom, which of course seemed like a terrible mismatch (and indeed <i>was</i>).&nbsp; This isn't a criticism so much as an acknowledgment of how it seems that Marvel loves pitting random heroes and villains together just to see what sparks fly.&nbsp; In this case, it flowed fairly well and had <i>most</i> of the elements that make this magazine good.\n\nBut there were some weak spots as well.&nbsp; Questions begin forming on the very first page, such as:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Why was Peter at a bowling alley with his classmates?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Since when has he ever gotten along with them?)]\n[BR] It's a tiny moment, but it signals that the story won't always stop to justify its choices.\n\nDoctor Doom appears almost immediately, and Stan has to take a moment to show readers how he escaped his fall at the end of [LINK:1963:FF_17|FF #17].&nbsp; (This was <i>essential</i> since that was indeed the last time readers saw him, only two months ago.)\n\n[ASSET: right | SpiderCommunicator | width=32%] One of the things that surprised me was how Doom banked on being able to send a signal out to Spider-Man via a captured spider.\n[BR]\n[BR] The Chameleon was <i>also</i> shown to have sent out such a signal (back in [LINK:1962:ASM_1_asm1|ASM #1]) that Spider-Man picked up, so this is twice in five issues (six if you include [LINK:1962:AMAZFANT_15_asm0|AMAZFANT #15]) that a villain has decided that Spider-Man is somehow able to pick up these types of waves.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=But when has it ever been stated that Spider-Man is able to communicate with spiders (the way Ant-Man communicates with ants)?]&nbsp; I don't <i>ever</i> recall reading anything of the sort.&nbsp; It feels like Marvel is still testing the boundaries of what \"spider‑powers\" might include before the concept is fully settled.\n\nAnd of course... Spider-Man has no trouble <i>following the signal</i> back to its origin point.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Ooooookay then!]\n\nFor my money, it only goes to show that, in this early point in the character's history, Stan and Steve probably haven't finished locking things down on exactly how these spider-powers work.&nbsp; (And as other early Marvels show -- particularly with Thor and his Swiss Army Knife Uru Hammer over in <i>Journey Into Mystery </i>-- it's still a work-in-progress.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut early characterization works for both Marvel heroes <i>and </i>villains, and Stan hilariously deals out the tried and true recruitment card that every megalomaniac has tucked up his sleeve, complete with the caption:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"Together, we can rule the <i>world</i>!\"] (all while secretly plotting to \"destroy him without a second thought\" -- <i>how typical!</i>)\n[ASSET: center | RuleTheWorld | width=67%]\n\nThis is the Silver Age equivalent of a stranger walking up to you and saying,\n[BR] \"Nice shoes.&nbsp; Want to overthrow civilization?\"\n\nThis elicited some laughter on my part, particularly when Spider-Man retorts about how a team-up would certainly be a conversation starter with JJJ!&nbsp; <i>Hahahaha!</i>\n\nBut after thinking it over... maybe for a couple of <i>microseconds</i>... Spider-Man declines the offer.\n[BR] Now enemies, commence brief battle scene!&nbsp; Spider-Man gets away... for now.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=I think you've already figured out that my reaction to having Dr. Doom appear as a Spider-Man villain strikes me as... unlikely.&nbsp; It's a difficult thing to imagine, and I'm trying to embrace the absurdity that was likely intended by the co-creators (at least in my opinion).&nbsp; It seems even <i>Spider-Man</i> views the whole thing as some sort of cosmic practical joke.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nHere's where I have to hand it to the co-creators since the notion of Doom mistaking Flash Thompson for the real Spider-Man because Peter was only feet away -- [SPAN: severity=success; text=that was a clever twist] -- one of the few plot turns in the issue that feels genuinely earned.\n[ASSET: center | PlotTwist | width=67%]\n\nBut I didn't find Doom's hijacking of the TV airwaves quite as realistic.&nbsp; Why broadcast over the air when last year's [LINK:1962:FF_5|FF #5] clearly demonstrated that he could fly his helicopter directly over the Baxter Building and shout down at his chosen prey?&nbsp; I know that the televised image of the captured Spider-Man was a new wrinkle that wasn't there during their first encounter, but Doom's message in <i>this</i> issue only says that the FF have one hour to <i>respond</i> to his ultimatum.\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=But then he provides <i>no information</i> on how to contact him!]\n[BR] Are they just supposed to magically <i>know</i> this?&nbsp; You'd <i>think</i> this would be an important detail!\n[BR] It's almost as if Doom assumes the world will simply rearrange itself around his demands.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(For the sake of completeness, we do at least learn on page 20 that Reed whipped up a \"sensor probe dial\" to zero in on Doom's location.)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | FreeFromFlash | width=33%] I loved the way Steve drew the panel where Peter contemplates the <i>delicious </i>prospect of sitting by, doing nothing, and allowing Dr. Doom to remove his \"Flash Thompson\" problem once and for all.\n[BR]\n[BR] (But we all knew he could never do such a thing -- not after he stood idly by and allowed the burglar that killed his uncle to pass by him untouched.)\n\nBut while I appreciated the artwork, [SPAN: severity=error; text=the narration and dialogue are problematic].&nbsp; The previous page indicates (quite clearly) that Peter just arrived home, <i>presumably</i> from school, just having missed being captured by Dr. Doom.&nbsp; He arrives home \"<b>minutes later</b>\" and finds Aunt May watching the Ed Sullivan show, but wasn't that show on Sunday evenings at 8 PM Eastern for the bulk of its run?&nbsp; The story makes it look like Peter just arrived home from school -- <i>on a <b>Sunday</b></i>?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Huh?]\n\nNot only that, but <i>right after Doom's announcement on TV</i>, Liz calls and says that Flash was dressed in a Spider-Man costume \"<b>last night</b>.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=What?]&nbsp; A whole day has now passed?&nbsp; (I'm not buying this, nor do I understand why Liz would even call Peter.&nbsp; Of all people -- Peter Parker?&nbsp; This does not compute!)&nbsp; As Peter rightly tells her \"Flash doesn't exactly head my list of favorite people.\"&nbsp; (Very true.)\n\nYou may think this is major nitpicking, but the timing problem is [SPAN: severity=error; text=right there on the page].&nbsp; I <b>get</b> that Silver Age pacing can be fast and loose, but this one <i>genuinely</i> strains the timeline.&nbsp; It's the kind of continuity wobble that jumps out once you really read these issues closely.\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | ActionToCome | width=13%] With the help of his spider senses, the hero somehow detects \"hostile emanations\" coming from a building.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=Really?]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Another example stretching the spider‑sense to fit whatever the plot needs.)]\n[BR]\n[BR] Page 13 promises <i>action</i>, even describing the fight to come as the\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"gol-dangest, ding-bustedest, rip-snortin'est\" fight you've ever seen!\"] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Talk about <i>overselling </i>it!)]\n\nAnd indeed, the next six pages feature a one-on-one battle between Spider-Man and Doctor Doom (with a little Doom robot action sprinkled in as well).&nbsp; I found it interesting that Spider-Man's confident talk at the beginning of the battle gradually shifted to [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"I'm in serious trouble!\"] as Doom's devices became more and more threatening.\n\n[ASSET: left | MoreExperiencedDoom | width=23%] Ditko does his best to sell the escalation, but the matchup itself is plainly lopsided, and indeed, the \"more experienced\" Doctor Doom begins to prevail (which any reader with half a brain could have predicted), and only when the Fantastic Four arrive does Doom finally decide he can't take on both them <i>and</i> Spider-Man at the same time.\n[BR]\n[BR] Perhaps Spider-Man had exhausted all his gadgets, or perhaps this was simply the issue that acknowledges Doom belongs in a different weight class.&nbsp; Regardless, he runs off, as does Spider-Man, seeing as he left Aunt May all alone in a dark house back on page 11 (which is how he had managed to justify his exit).\n\n[ASSET: right | SpiderManWasHere | width=11%] That left only time enough for the Thing to grab Flash Thompson, as everyone else had fled.\n[BR]\n[BR] (And I <i>love</i> that he wanted to <i>lean on him</i> \"just a little\" for what he considered a complete waste of time.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=<i>Heh</i>, perfectly in character!])\n\nThe FF finally having made their cameo appearance, Peter Parker returns home to find Aunt May okay.&nbsp; But Jameson berates him for not snapping any photos, even while Betty Brant indicates that <i>she</i> thinks he's \"wonderful!\"&nbsp; And the issue closes with Peter having to listen to Flash Thompson bragging about his exaggerated encounter with Doctor Doom.\n\nThe Parker luck holds true to form:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=all bad!]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt seems clear right from the get-go that this month's issue was created to showcase what seemed like an obvious mismatch.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Spider-Man versus Doctor Doom?]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=(Really?)]\n\nI'm <i>sure</i> that the cover was intended to generate sales (not that they really needed it, as <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> obviously had good enough sales figures to have now gone monthly, this being its first monthly issue, in fact).\n\nBut once you get into the story, you find a host of puzzling questions:\n\n[HANG] * Peter hanging out with his classmates at a bowling alley?\n[HANG] * Doom using a spider to contact Spider-Man?\n[HANG] * Flash seemingly getting kidnapped after school... but on a <i>Sunday evening?</i>\n[HANG] * Liz randomly calling Peter Parker to inform him of Flash's disappearance \"last night?\"\n[HANG] * Doom broadcasting over the TV airwaves instead of direct communication with the FF?\n[HANG] * Peter leaving his Aunt May alone in a dark house?\n[HANG] * A building that has \"hostile emanations?\"\n\nThese are admittedly head-scratchers, and they do get in the way of the overall flow of the story, which was otherwise compelling in its own way.\n\nFlash's portrayal seemed true to form from start to finish.&nbsp; First he's revealed to be an honest-to-goodness Spider-Man fan.&nbsp; He tries to play a practical joke on Peter, but when he's kidnapped by Dr. Doom, he becomes genuinely fearful (not a surprise), but once back at school, he boasts to everyone about his encounter.&nbsp; All this is totally in character.\n\nAs for Doctor Doom, he seemed a bit more petty in this issue than normal.&nbsp; We learn that he's still fixated on the Fantastic Four, and Spider-Man was only a pawn in that over-arching scheme.&nbsp; The surprise of the title hero at the proposed team-up was believable (which I liked), but the several pages of battle action seemed to exist only to justify Doom's presence in this magazine.\n\n(I would think readers would prefer to see Doom and the <i>Thing</i> go one-on-one.&nbsp; Spider-Man seemed out of his depth -- but <i>that was likely the <b>whole point</b></i>.)\n\nAnd this being a Spider-Man issue, Stan and Steve seemed to have felt obliged to cram Jameson and Betty Brant into the mix, and at least for this month, it came across a bit as overcrowding.&nbsp; There was enough story to have omitted them this time around.\n\nDespite all its hiccups, the issue was still worth reading and got my attention right off the bat.&nbsp; The host of head-scratching moments were periodically distracting, but the notion of a Spider-Man/Doctor Doom team-up (or even battle) was intriguing enough to make the experience worthwhile.\n\nUsing Flash Thompson as a foil to force the final showdown was well-done, and what reader didn't like the brief appearance of the Fantastic Four in the end?&nbsp; (The sight of the Thing holding up Flash Thompson, wanting to lean on him \"just a little\" was a golden moment.)\n\nAll these things add up to a story that admittedly won't take home any prizes... but was still worth the time invested.&nbsp; It was uneven at times, but never dull -- and that's often the charm of these early outings.\n\n<u>Final thought:</u>\n[BR] Does Doom decide to scrap his Spider-Man-tracking gadget because it seems to have failed him?\n[BR] Will we ever see it again?&nbsp; (Who can say?)",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * You can't help but admire J. Jonah Jameson for his attempts to make Spider-Man look bad every chance he gets -- even to the point of buying television time to air a special (and we learn on page 8 that JJJ's <i>real</i> motivation is money -- \"Spider-Man <i>sells papers</i>!\").\n\n[HANG0] * Miss Brant is given a first name for the first time right on the splash page, which is not actually mentioned in the story until the last couple of panels, which hints at more to come in a possible relationship between her and Peter."
  ],
  "ownership": "[ASSET: right | JeffWithASM5 | width=11%] I bought a pretty decent copy of this book in the year 2000 (not part of the \"2000 collection\" though) for $245 and then paid nearly $85 more to have it professionally graded at CGC, coming back with a grade of 5.5.&nbsp; The book ultimately sold for $1009 on ebay in May 2019, netting me a nice profit of over $515, which still ranks in my top-10 in terms of individual profit margin for a single book.\n\nWhile it may sell for more <i>today</i>, at the <i>time</i>, this was a very good price (and honestly more than I had bargained on).&nbsp; It remains one of only a handful for which I grossed over one grand.",
  "highlight": "Spider‑Man vs. Doctor Doom delivers clever moments and head‑scratchers in equal measure."
}